# Unit Testing and Coverage

This one is pretty straightforward, we recommend [pytest](https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/) as a testing framework, and use [coverage](https://coverage.readthedocs.io/en/latest/index.html), as well as a [coverage plugin for pytest](https://pypi.org/project/pytest-cov/) to generate coverage (statement / branch) reports every time the tests are run.

Note that in the coverage settings in the `pyproject.toml`, you can specify a coverage threshold under which the tests will fail. This is to encourage a thorough and organic testing strategy. Don't test as an afterthought, but rather consider this as a non-negotiable part of the development cost.

When building the documentation, both the latest testing and coverage reports are used and uploaded as documentation artifacts visible to anyone.

Your coverage report will be available [here]({{ docs_url }}/latest/exported/coverage/) and your test results will be available [here]({{ docs_url }}/latest/exported/pytest.html?sort=result)

## Testing across Python versions (nox)

`uv run poe test` runs the suite on a single interpreter and is the canonical run that produces the coverage report and the badges/artifacts embedded in the docs. To make sure your code works across *all* supported Python versions, this project also uses [nox](https://nox.thea.codes/):

```bash
uv run poe test-all      # = nox -s tests
```

`noxfile.py` declares the supported versions (3.10–3.13) and is the single source of truth for that list — CI runs the same `nox` session rather than duplicating the version matrix. Each session creates an isolated environment (using uv as the backend) and runs the suite without the coverage/artifact options, so the matrix is a fast pass/fail correctness check while `poe test` handles coverage.
